\]

Here, each element of \( S_L \) consists of the pair \( \langle \alpha_n, n \rangle \) where \( \alpha_n \) is the angel string for strings of length \( n \) and \( n \) identifies the length of strings considered. Since \( \alpha_n \) is of polynomial length \( p(n) \), and \( n \) is just a unary representation or similarly small encoding, this ensures \( S_L \) is sparse.

The role of the angel string in this construction is fundamental: it provides the specific information needed by the Turing Machine \( M \) to decide membership for any string \( x \) of length \( n \). The set \( S_L \) effectively acts as a record of angel strings indexed by their applicable lengths.

