[INFO] 10-01 16:26:38 execution.py:378] To construct a single sparse set \( S \) from \( k \) sparse sets \( S_1, S_2, \ldots, S_k \), we can define \( S \) as follows:

Define the set \( S \subseteq \Sigma^* \) such that for any string \( x \), \( (x, i) \in S \) if and only if \( x \in S_i \) and \( 1 \leq i \leq k \). In other words, we pair each string \( x \) from a sparse set \( S_i \) with its index \( i \) and include the pair \((x, i)\) in \( S \).

The construction of \( S \) is as follows:

\[ S = \bigcup_{i=1}^k \{ (x, i) \mid x \in S_i \} \]

Now, we need to verify that \( S \) itself is sparse. Since each \( S_i \) is sparse, there exists a polynomial \( p_i(n) \) such that for any \( n \), the number of strings of length \( n \) in \( S_i \) is bounded by \( p_i(n) \). The total number of strings of length \( n \) in each \( S_i \) is then:

\[ |S_i^{=n}| \leq p_i(n) \]

Since \( S \) includes a bounded number of strings from each \( S_i \) appended with a constant-size index \( i \), the number of strings of a given length in \( S \) can be bounded by:

\[ |S^{=n+k}| \leq \sum_{i=1}^k |S_i^{=n}| \leq \sum_{i=1}^k p_i(n) \]

Therefore, \( S \) is sparse because the number of strings of a given length is a sum of polynomials, which is itself a polynomial.

This way, \( S \) can be used by a deterministic polynomial-time Turing Machine with oracle access to determine membership in any \( S_i \): given input \((x, i)\), the TM queries the oracle whether \((x, i) \in S\), which is equivalent to checking if \( x \in S_i \).
